73 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 61 0 R
/E (A transaction allows you to group multiple SQL statements into a single unit of work. If any part of the sequence fails, the entire transaction is rolled back, leaving the database in its original, clean state. This is critical for financial records, inventory management, and any logic where partial updates would result in corrupted data.)
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
74 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 61 0 R
/E (The Golden Rule:)
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
75 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 61 0 R
/E (Never perform long-running "Business Logic" \(like calling an external API\) inside a transaction. Keep transactions as short as possible to minimize row locking, which can lead to performance bottlenecks and deadlocks in a high-concurrency system.)
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
